      "extract": {
        "binary": false,
        "lines": 308,
        "headings": [
          {
            "line": 1,
            "level": 1,
            "text": "Building a real Jarvis: the definitive desktop agent architecture guide"
          },
          {
            "line": 7,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "A. The correct architecture is planner-executor-verifier, not a single loop"
          },
          {
            "line": 37,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "B. Capability design: MCP servers and hybrid API-GUI execution"
          },
          {
            "line": 62,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "C. GUI interaction: UIA-first with surgical vision fallback"
          },
          {
            "line": 86,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "D. The best local models for a 16GB VRAM desktop agent"
          },
          {
            "line": 90,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Primary recommendation: Qwen3 14B"
          },
          {
            "line": 94,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Strong alternative: Mistral Small 3.1 24B"
          },
          {
            "line": 98,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Best instruction follower: Phi-4-Reasoning 14B"
          },
          {
            "line": 102,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Dual-brain configuration that fits 16GB"
          },
          {
            "line": 112,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Models to avoid for desktop agents"
          },
          {
            "line": 116,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Hybrid local + cloud"
          },
          {
            "line": 122,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "E. Runtime recommendation: llama.cpp server for maximum control"
          },
          {
            "line": 124,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "The winner for Windows desktop agents: llama-server"
          },
          {
            "line": 143,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Runner-up: LM Studio"
          },
          {
            "line": 147,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Ollama: fine for prototyping, suboptimal for production"
          },
          {
            "line": 151,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "TabbyAPI for maximum quality-per-bit"
          },
          {
            "line": 155,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "vLLM and SGLang: not for Windows"
          },
          {
            "line": 161,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "F. Memory: start with SQLite, not vector databases"
          },
          {
            "line": 165,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Minimal viable memory (build this first)"
          },
          {
            "line": 183,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Skill library pattern (from OS-Copilot/FRIDAY)"
          },
          {
            "line": 187,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "Learning from failures without context pollution"
          },
          {
            "line": 191,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "When to add vector search"
          },
          {
            "line": 197,
            "level": 2,
            "text": "G. Voice pipeline: achieving conversational Jarvis under 11 GB VRAM"
          },
          {
            "line": 201,
            "level": 3,
            "text": "TTS: Kokoro for the best quality-to-VRAM ratio"
          }
        ],
        "lead": "**The core problem with CARTER OS isn't the LLM — it's the architecture.** Modern desktop agents that achieve human-level performance (Agent S3 at 72.6% on OSWorld, UFO2 across 20+ Windows apps) share a decisive structural pattern: they separate planning from execution, wrap LLM decisions in deterministic state machines, and treat GUI interaction as a last resort behind native APIs. Your agent fai",
        "measures": [
          {
            "line": 64,
            "text": "The debate between UI Automation and vision-first approaches is settled. **Hybrid UIA-first is definitively superior**, and UFO2 proved it with 10% higher task completion than the best vision-only agents."
          },
          {
            "line": 70,
            "text": "**The optimal perception pipeline for 16GB VRAM**:"
          },
          {
            "line": 72,
            "text": "1. **Screenshot capture** (~5ms) via Windows.Graphics.Capture API"
          },
          {
            "line": 73,
            "text": "2. **Change detection** (~2ms) via pixel diff against cached frame — skip re-analysis if <5% changed"
          },
          {
            "line": 74,
            "text": "3. **UIA tree query** (~10–50ms) for the active window — get all visible, enabled controls with labels and bounding boxes"
          },
          {
            "line": 75,
            "text": "4. **If UIA covers the task** (most standard apps): send control list to text LLM → LLM selects action by control ID → execute via pywinauto. Total: **<100ms perception**"
          },
          {
            "line": 76,
            "text": "5. **If UIA is insufficient**: run OCR on screenshot (~50–200ms via PaddleOCR on CPU or Windows OCR API at zero VRAM cost)"
          },
          {
            "line": 77,
            "text": "6. **If still insufficient**: run **OmniParser v2** (~0.8s, ~2–3 GB VRAM) — YOLO detector + Florence-2 captioner detects all interactable elements invisible to UIA"
          },
          {
            "line": 106,
            "text": "- **Fast brain**: Phi-4-mini 3.8B at Q4 (~3.5 GB) — handles simple routing, classification, basic tool calls with native function calling support"
          },
          {
            "line": 107,
            "text": "- **Complex brain**: Qwen3 14B at Q4 (~9.2 GB) — handles multi-step reasoning, code generation, complex planning"
          },
          {
            "line": 108,
            "text": "- **Total**: ~12.7 GB, leaving headroom for KV cache and OmniParser"
          },
          {
            "line": 129,
            "text": "- **Best structured output**: GBNF grammar system with JSON schema auto-conversion guarantees 100% valid JSON"
          }
        ],
